# TTOS CTF bench rig -- pin the two PCAN-USB FD adapters to ROLE names.
#
# WHY ROLE NAMES AND NOT can0/can1:
# A test that says "observe ttdiag" cannot be silently backwards, whatever the
# cabling happens to be. That mattered more than expected: the roles were assigned
# here the other way round until 2026-08-03, following a claim that the DUT's can1
# was the drive bus. It was not -- the drive harness lands on the CAN0 terminal on
# a real car, and the Pi's config now matches. Only these two lines changed; no
# cable moved. That is the whole point of naming by role.
#
# arcana 3-6.3 is wired to the DUT's can1 (DIAG), 3-6.4 to the DUT's can0 (DRIVE).
#
# A THIRD adapter goes to a complete vehicle's diagnostic port -- `ttcardiag`. The
# DUT rig is for unit and functional work with emulated nodes; the car is the only
# place the real firmware, the real drivetrain and the real bus timing exist.
#
# NOTE ON SWAPPING ROLES: udev cannot rename two interfaces into each other's names
# -- the first rename fails because the target name is still taken, and it fails
# SILENTLY, leaving the old mapping in place. If you invert these lines, rename
# through a temporary name by hand or reboot. That bit once already.
#
# The DUT keeps its own can0/can1 names (DRIVE=can0, DIAG=can1 there) because
# those are baked into the shipped .network files and the dashboard config. The
# bench is the only place these names exist, so the two never have to agree --
# which is the point. Read every bench script as roles, never as numbers.
#
# WHY ID_PATH AND NOT SERIAL:
# The doc says to pin by adapter serial. PEAK PCAN-USB FD publishes no USB
# iSerial -- /sys/bus/usb/devices/*/serial does not exist for them, so there is
# nothing to match on. ID_PATH (the physical USB port) is the available stable
# key. Consequence: THE ROLE FOLLOWS THE PORT, NOT THE ADAPTER. Swap which port
# a cable is plugged into and the roles swap with it.
#
# bench-up.sh re-verifies the mapping against live bus traffic on every run, so a
# mis-plug is caught before any assertion runs rather than inverting its result.
#
# Install:  sudo cp 99-ttos-bench-can.rules /etc/udev/rules.d/
#           sudo udevadm control --reload && sudo udevadm trigger -s net

SUBSYSTEM=="net", ACTION=="add", ENV{ID_PATH}=="pci-0000:06:00.3-usb-0:6.3:1.0", NAME="ttdiag"
SUBSYSTEM=="net", ACTION=="add", ENV{ID_PATH}=="pci-0000:06:00.3-usb-0:6.4:1.0", NAME="ttdrive"
SUBSYSTEM=="net", ACTION=="add", ENV{ID_PATH}=="pci-0000:0a:00.3-usb-0:3:1.0", NAME="ttcardiag"
